WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:08.560
CNN: It spans the globe like a super highway,
it is called “Internet.”

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.230
David Bowie: I think we’re actually on the
cusp of something exhilarating and terrifying.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:14.250
Jeremy Paxman: It’s just a tool though isn’t
it?

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:16.630
David Bowie: No, it’s not, no.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:18.619
It’s an alien life form.

00:00:18.619 --> 00:00:22.840
ABC Science: It’s going to get woven into
the daily fabric of our lives.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.619
It will just be like using a telephone, it’ll
be like using your car on the roads, we take

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:27.619
it for granted.

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:31.930
If I wanted to talk to someone on the far
side of the world it would be just as natural

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:33.950
as me talking to you now.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:35.550
SIERRA: The Internet.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:41.320
What was once a marvel is now something our
modern world depends on for, well, everything.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.970
It allows us access.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:49.820
Access to crucial information, not-so-crucial
information, and access to each other.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:52.760
But not everyone’s access is created equally.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:01:00.750
In fact, it may no longer be accurate to think
of THE internet as a single entity.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:08.090
That’s because around the world, different
governments have taken very different approaches

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:14.400
to regulating the internet, shaping what their
citizens access online and how their private

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.040
data is tracked.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.450
And this has led to a splintering.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:25.480
The world now has three primary “internets”:
One led by the United States, one led by China,

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.190
and one led by the European Union.

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:33.560
And if things keep going as they are, we could
be headed toward an even more divided digital

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.560
world.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.320
My name is Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why
It Matters.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:44.250
Today the world’s three internets and what
they mean for our future.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:48.040
SEGAL: For most users, they still see one
internet, right?

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.619
You're going to go on the internet, you're
going to search for whatever it's going to

00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:54.860
be, K-pop band, and you're going to see all
the highlights and you're going to get all

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:56.000
that stuff.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.360
SIERRA: This is Adam Segal.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.399
He directs the Digital and Cyberspace Policy
Program here at the Council.

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:07.860
He recently led an independent task force
which further details U.S. national security

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.690
innovations for defending an open and secure
internet.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:16.980
SEGAL: But what we're beginning to see is
that governments are regulating and thinking

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.580
about how the internet should be regulated
very differently.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.580
And there are three major models.

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:27.920
There's the way that the United States has
done it, there is the way that China has done

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.630
it from the beginning, which is much more
restrictive.

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:35.440
And then there's a European model that kind
of blends the American focus on human rights

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.740
and openness with more of a tendency towards
government regulation.

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:44.710
WHEELER: The nature of what we're talking
about when we say three internets is a regulatory

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:50.740
environment, a climate, and some degree of
authoritative control over how that information

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:51.740
flows.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.330
SIERRA: This is Tarah Wheeler.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:59.310
She is Senior Fellow for Global Cyber Policy
here at the Council, and CEO of Red Queen

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.720
Dynamics, a cybersecurity company.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.910
WHEELER: A lot of the question around what
constitutes an internet is who is allowed

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:11.020
to listen to what you're doing and do something
about it, and how much of your time do you

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.660
have to put into enabling that for that third
party?

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:17.870
That's really what it kind of boils down to
is what is this regime, what does this internet

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:23.190
think it has the right and responsibility
to do with you as an internet user?

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:27.519
Whether that's by turning off the tap, literally
severing a transatlantic cable, or simply

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:32.700
because a government can control the internet
service providers inside their territory and

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:34.300
mandate that they shut those off.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.720
We see that there are three different real
manifestations of what constitutes an internet

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.310
in the world.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:46.451
SIERRA: So that means that if there are three
people, one in New York, one in Beijing, one

00:03:46.451 --> 00:03:52.490
in Berlin, and they all look up the same thing,
let's say K-Pop, their experiences are going

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:55.170
to be different based on where they are geographically?

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.590
SEGAL: Yes.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:03:59.750
Not with K-pop, K-pop probably they get all
the same answers.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:04.220
But, the classic example with China is, you
look up Tank Man.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:08.470
Tank Man is the picture of the individual
standing in front of a line of tanks after

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:12.890
the People's Liberation Army cracked down
on the democracy movement in Tiananmen in

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.810
1989.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:18.500
If you Google that in the United States and
Europe, you get that image, you get to see

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:19.500
that picture.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:24.419
You Google that in China, you don't get anything,
because the Chinese don't want young Chinese

00:04:24.419 --> 00:04:28.580
to know about Tiananmen happening, and so
that image doesn't exist in China.

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:29.639
It's controlled.

00:04:29.639 --> 00:04:33.290
If you Google Tiananmen in China, you just
get a picture of beautiful Tiananmen and probably

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:37.080
some PLA soldier raising the flag in the morning.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:41.690
Similarly, if you were to Google Mein Kampf
the book in the United States, you would get

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:45.740
the Wikipedia entry and you'd probably get
a link to buy it on Amazon.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:49.449
But in a lot of European states, and in Germany
in particular, you would get the Wikipedia

00:04:49.449 --> 00:04:52.220
link, but you would not get the link to buy
Mein Kampf, right?

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:56.460
Because you can't buy Nazi paraphernalia in
Germany because they have restrictions on

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.130
that, because of historical reasons.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:03.389
SIERRA: And not only will your search results
vary, the information collected on you as

00:05:03.389 --> 00:05:06.190
you search will also change.

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:12.130
This is a lot to digest, so let’s dig deeper
into each of these internets, starting with

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:13.310
the United States model.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:19.080
PBS NewsHour: Google, Facebook, Amazon, Apple,
are among the most powerful monopolies in

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:20.080
the history of humanity.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.940
Wall Street Journal: They develop these products
and services that have made such a deep impact

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:28.260
into the lives of so many people on an everyday
basis.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:33.340
CNBC: Let’s, meanwhile, note that Apple
just hit the three trillion dollar mark in

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.340
total market cap.

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:39.560
Al Jazeera: After months of uncertainty and
a long legal battle, the world’s wealthiest

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:44.120
man has now taken charge of one of the most
influential social media platforms.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:48.509
Senator Orrin Hatch: How do you sustain a
business model in which users don’t pay

00:05:48.509 --> 00:05:49.509
for your service?

00:05:49.509 --> 00:05:50.630
Mark Zuckerburg: Senator, we run ads.

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:57.650
SEGAL: The United States internet is driven
by two big principles.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:03.390
The First Amendment, so free speech, and then
a very, very light regulatory hand from the

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:04.390
government.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:09.000
So the internet sector was not taxed at the
beginning, it was not regulated for the most

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.000
part.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.100
And so it grew because of that, and it allowed
for this free exchange of ideas.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:17.120
But the U.S. is unique in having a First Amendment.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.840
Other democracies don't have a First Amendment.

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.880
Other democracies have what they consider
legitimate reasons to control speech because

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:28.520
of concerns about communal violence or because
of their history.

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:32.759
So from an innovative perspective, I think
there's little doubt that the U.S. is still

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:33.759
driving it.

00:06:33.759 --> 00:06:37.750
Although we are beginning to see the emergence
of Chinese tech platforms, TikTok being the

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:41.419
big one that no one really expected to see.

00:06:41.419 --> 00:06:43.880
But innovative perspective is still there.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:50.080
There has been over the last five years a
real sense of the information the tech firms

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:57.130
have about us, and how they use it in ways
that we don't necessarily control, plus the

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:59.669
kind of negative social impacts, right?

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:05.729
So how that plays on teenagers and others
who are constantly checking in and all these

00:07:05.729 --> 00:07:06.729
other things.

00:07:06.729 --> 00:07:10.700
From a political perspective, what we see
is both the right and the left, the Democrats

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:14.289
and the Republicans are very unhappy for different
reasons.

00:07:14.289 --> 00:07:23.889
The Democrats and the Left have become unhappy
both because of the rise of misogyny and transphobia

00:07:23.889 --> 00:07:31.020
and racism and the fact that a lot of these
platforms have become places for abuse.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:37.270
Now from the Right's perspective, they are
worried about tech’s control over free speech.

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:43.570
And so a lot of the things that, when Twitter
or Facebook takes something down, the Right

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:46.800
tends to see it as stifling speech, free speech.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.490
So, the Right attacked Big Speech from that
perspective.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:54.830
And then the Right and the Left both are unhappy
with Big Tech because of anti monopoly, big

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:55.830
trust.

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:58.539
Do they dominate the markets too much?

00:07:58.539 --> 00:08:02.440
BRADFORD: So there's much good that has been
accomplished.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:08.169
But I think we are increasingly aware of the
downsides of the internet as well.

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:09.599
SIERRA: This is Anu Bradford.

00:08:09.599 --> 00:08:14.180
She is the Henry L. Moses Professor of Law
and International Organization at Columbia

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.250
Law School.

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:21.290
BRADFORD: The idea that it's not only a medium
to force the beneficial societal debates that

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:27.139
contribute towards more vibrant and participatory
democracy, the internet is also rampant with

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:34.029
disinformation that destabilizes our democracies
with hate speech that compromises our dignity.

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:38.810
Those are just some examples of how we've
sort of gotten both things, we've gotten the

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:44.480
good, but we've also gotten a lot of harmful
effects, with all those connections.

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:49.800
And also, I think nobody was able to predict
just how powerful these leading internet platforms

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:50.800
would become.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:55.740
So the kind of market power that these companies
have the kind of economic and political and

00:08:55.740 --> 00:09:01.190
cultural and societal influence they exert,
that is something that has really shaped societies

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:07.370
in a way that can be very different, what
sort of democratic societies would look like,

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:11.579
if that power was rooted in real sort of democratic
governance.

00:09:11.579 --> 00:09:15.779
Another concern is the privacy of individuals.

00:09:15.779 --> 00:09:23.329
SIERRA: According to Pew, 68% of internet
users believe current laws are not good enough

00:09:23.329 --> 00:09:26.080
in protecting people’s privacy online.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:31.480
Which kind of makes sense since the most important
law when it comes to the U.S. internet, called

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:36.279
Section 230, actually protects companies not
users.

00:09:36.279 --> 00:09:41.500
It frees companies like Facebook or Twitter
from liability for the content that its users

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:43.959
post on their platforms.

00:09:43.959 --> 00:09:50.220
Some experts have referred to Section 230
as “the 26 words that created the Internet,”

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:54.410
because it freed startups to move quickly
without fear of lawsuits.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:58.600
There are limited protections in the United
States for certain types of highly sensitive

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:02.300
information, like health data or social security
numbers.

00:10:02.300 --> 00:10:07.970
But there is no agency, or set of laws, that
were created exclusively to protect the digital

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:09.970
privacy of Americans.

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:15.079
However, if you live in states like California
or Connecticut, you may benefit from state-level

00:10:15.079 --> 00:10:16.220
data protections.

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:18.450
But overall, it’s sort of a mess.

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:22.410
BRADFORD: So, our lives have become exposed
to these companies that are in a position

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:29.190
to extract our data and commercialize that
data in ways that really deprives individuals

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.019
of their privacy.

00:10:31.019 --> 00:10:36.339
And one could say to sort of compromise the
autonomy and ability to make decisions, when

00:10:36.339 --> 00:10:40.660
that data can be used, then, to manipulate
the choices that we make online.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:47.329
SIERRA: Social psychologist Shoshana Zuboff
coined a term for this: ‘surveillance capitalism.’

00:10:47.329 --> 00:10:54.389
Basically, U.S. corporations claim private
human experiences as free raw data.

00:10:54.389 --> 00:10:59.470
So, the same phone that you use to navigate
to your favorite restaurant tracks your every

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:03.149
move, often when you’re not even using your
GPS.

00:11:03.149 --> 00:11:08.160
Every key word you search, every link you
click, and every purchase you make goes into

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:11.470
algorithms that shape your consumer profile.

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:14.100
This can feel icky, even spooky.

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:17.500
And it's not the only problem that Americans
face online.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:23.000
There’s also a steady stream of toxic, polarizing,
and misleading content.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:27.750
WHEELER: The internet does feel more toxic
right now.

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:33.200
We do talk about people, women, people who
are non-binary, people of color getting bullied,

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:34.980
experiencing this toxicity out there.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:39.670
But on a lot of levels, that comes as a result
of the democratization of the internet.

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:42.910
We let the assholes onto the internet now
too, and not just the nerds.

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.670
SIERRA: Ah, our first ever censorship bleep.

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:47.160
And on the perfect episode.

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:48.610
You gotta love that.

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:53.420
WHEELER: So a lot of the problem we're having
right now is just that it's people using communication

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:54.519
technologies.

00:11:54.519 --> 00:11:59.829
So it's going to be an interesting challenge
to see how we handle this toxicity, this problem

00:11:59.829 --> 00:12:05.320
of letting just anybody in here without limiting
speech, without limiting the communication

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:06.750
protocols themselves.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:08.470
SIERRA: Okay, fair.

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:13.230
But do you think it's an easier lift to figure
out how to regulate the place where this is

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:17.590
all happening versus making all humans good
humans?

00:12:17.590 --> 00:12:24.230
WHEELER: I think the challenge that we’re
having here is human beings, and the way they

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:28.769
interact with technology often doesn’t really
take into account the fact that they’re

00:12:28.769 --> 00:12:29.910
dealing with real people.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:33.060
So, is it easier to regulate the internet?

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:39.800
Not without some massive unintended consequences
that I think are beyond the ken, understanding,

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:43.209
and desire of the same people who want to
regulate the internet.

00:12:43.209 --> 00:12:47.760
You can't ban toxic speech without banning
speech, and you can't ban toxic speech without

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:48.930
defining it.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:52.500
And there's always going to be people that
think toxic speech is different than your

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:53.889
own definition.

00:12:53.889 --> 00:13:00.630
SIERRA: Why is the U.S. so slow or reluctant
to create laws and regulations that curtail

00:13:00.630 --> 00:13:04.190
the parts of this that everyone seems so unhappy
with?

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:06.680
SEGAL: Yeah, I think it's because we were
so successful.

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:07.680
Right?

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:13.949
I think the internet grew so fast, and created
such wealth, and became global and is just

00:13:13.949 --> 00:13:16.510
this amazing American success story, right?

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:21.000
It was created by DARPA, the Defense Agency
Research Project.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:25.510
So it is this amazing kind of American invention
that became a global platform.

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:27.620
And we were really afraid to mess it up.

00:13:27.620 --> 00:13:34.410
People thought that the regulation would stifle
innovation, and there were real First Amendment

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:37.800
concerns that are still making it really hard
to figure out what we're going to do about

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:40.199
content in the United States.

00:13:40.199 --> 00:13:46.370
And then I think there was also some hubris
that everybody would just accept the American

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:48.570
model, and realize that that was in their
best interest.

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:53.889
And I have been in several meetings with U.S.
government officials with a counterpart either

00:13:53.889 --> 00:13:58.990
for example, Indian or Chinese for that matter,
where the U.S. tries to lecture to the other

00:13:58.990 --> 00:13:59.990
side.

00:13:59.990 --> 00:14:02.940
Like ‘no, no, no, a global open internet
is in your best interest.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:08.490
You're going to lose out on the economic benefits
if you try to control the internet.’

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:12.740
And those countries were like, well one, who
are you to be lecturing us on our own interest?

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:13.740
And two, you're wrong.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:16.899
We can figure it out about how to control
the internet and still grow.

00:14:16.899 --> 00:14:21.320
And the Chinese have, the Chinese have managed
to do that pretty well.

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:25.880
SIERRA: Okay, so that’s the American internet:
overflowing with innovation and profit; but

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:28.920
very light on regulations and privacy.

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:35.130
And that leads us to the other end of the
spectrum: China, home to roughly 1 billion

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:39.600
internet users, and the most regulated internet
system on Earth.

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:43.220
TIME: China’s great firewall just became
even bigger.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:46.640
Blocking the free flow of information on the
internet even further.

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:51.550
CNN Business: War is already playing out between
the U.S. and China on the technological battlefield.

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:56.829
PBS NewsHour: Huawei and other Chinese telecom
companies are building 5G and smart cities

00:14:56.829 --> 00:14:58.199
in more than 65 countries.

00:14:58.199 --> 00:15:02.440
Rachel Martin: China is cracking down on mass
protests that broke out over the weekend.

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:07.459
Emily Fang: They’re stopping random people
and checking their phones for apps like telegram

00:15:07.459 --> 00:15:08.920
and instagram.

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:10.839
All mentions of them are being deleted online.

00:15:10.839 --> 00:15:15.899
And there has been no official acknowledgement
that these demonstrations even happened over

00:15:15.899 --> 00:15:18.149
the weekend.

00:15:18.149 --> 00:15:22.880
SEGAL: From the very beginning, when China
connected to the internet, they thought of

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:24.660
it as a dual edge sword.

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:30.100
So they knew that the internet was going to
be extremely important for economic development,

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:35.310
but they from the beginning were very worried
about the flow of information from the outside

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:41.240
into China that they couldn't control, that
would threaten the legitimacy and the stability

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:43.269
of the Communist Party.

00:15:43.269 --> 00:15:48.019
So what the Chinese did at the very beginning
was block information from the outside, which

00:15:48.019 --> 00:15:50.930
we refer to or know as ‘The Great Firewall.’

00:15:50.930 --> 00:15:52.550
So, blocking information.

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:56.040
And they blocked U.S. companies that wouldn't
play by those rules.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:02.740
So we saw Google and Facebook trying to enter
the China market, but then basically having

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:09.040
to pull back because they were unwilling to
censor, or eventually the cost of censoring

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:11.589
became too much for them so they withdrew
from the market.

00:16:11.589 --> 00:16:16.769
SIERRA: Across the world, when you sign on
to the internet, you find the same companies

00:16:16.769 --> 00:16:20.630
- Google, Facebook, Amazon and the rest of
the American giants.

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:24.589
They may look a bit different based on location,
but they’re there.

00:16:24.589 --> 00:16:28.110
Once you cross over into China though, that
changes completely.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:31.170
Instead of Google, you’ll be searching on
Baidu.

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:35.459
Instead of Amazon, you’ll find yourself
shopping on Alibaba or TaoBao.

00:16:35.459 --> 00:16:39.079
For dating, instead of Tinder you may use
Momo or Tantan.

00:16:39.079 --> 00:16:44.630
And for social media, you’ll find yourself
using Weibo, QQ, or the incredibly popular

00:16:44.630 --> 00:16:45.630
WeChat.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:51.279
SEGAL: Again, for a Chinese user, if you're
interested in K-pop, the internet is fantastic.

00:16:51.279 --> 00:16:55.160
And the Chinese internet has had lots of innovations
that we haven't.

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:59.959
The big one being WeChat, which is a platform
that allows you to do almost everything.

00:16:59.959 --> 00:17:06.679
So it's a messenger, and a payment system,
and you can't get a cab without it any longer

00:17:06.679 --> 00:17:07.679
in China.

00:17:07.679 --> 00:17:09.250
And so it's everything in one place.

00:17:09.250 --> 00:17:13.520
And there have been lots OF breakthroughs
on services and things like that in the Chinese

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:14.520
case.

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:18.439
But one way to think about the Chinese internet
is, as we mentioned, The Great Firewall exists

00:17:18.439 --> 00:17:20.069
to keep things out.

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:24.569
And then, inside, there are just a lot of
controls to make sure people don't post things

00:17:24.569 --> 00:17:26.699
that are against the party.

00:17:26.699 --> 00:17:28.220
Real name registration.

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:32.831
Everybody has to register to their accounts,
their social media accounts with their real

00:17:32.831 --> 00:17:33.831
name.

00:17:33.831 --> 00:17:35.669
There's no anonymity like there is on Twitter.

00:17:35.669 --> 00:17:40.230
So, it makes it very easy to get arrested
or called in for questioning.

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:44.320
All the technology companies in China, social
media is responsible for what they post.

00:17:44.320 --> 00:17:49.549
So in the United States we have intermediate
liability under something called Section 230,

00:17:49.549 --> 00:17:53.471
which says the companies are allowed to take
some things down if they want to, but they're

00:17:53.471 --> 00:17:57.120
not legally responsible for what somebody
puts up, if it's not true.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:02.299
In China, they are responsible, and so they
hire tens of thousands of censors to take

00:18:02.299 --> 00:18:03.720
things down.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:08.100
And then you can be called in if you post
something that the Chinese government doesn't

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:12.330
like, you can be what's called, ‘called
in for tea,’ which means the local ministry

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:15.809
of public security comes by and says, don't
post that again.

00:18:15.809 --> 00:18:20.970
SIERRA: I can remember years ago when some
people thought that access to information

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:24.400
would undermine the ability of the communist
party to retain control.

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:26.350
But it doesn't look like that happened.

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:29.780
SEGAL: No, I mean, so there was a great quote
from President Clinton…

00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:33.520
Bill Clinton: Now there’s no question China
has been trying to crack down on the internet.

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:34.580
Good luck.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:39.890
That’s sort of like trying to nail jello
to the wall.

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:42.950
SEGAL: And it turns out that Chinese are pretty
good at nailing jello to the wall.

00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:47.270
So there is the question about what access
to information that they get.

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:50.430
But the other thing that we saw is that the
Chinese were not only concerned about the

00:18:50.430 --> 00:18:54.770
access to the information, they were really
concerned about organizing.

00:18:54.770 --> 00:19:01.730
So any time they saw any type of organization,
then they quickly shut everything down and

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:04.700
really made an example of those people in
real life.

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:10.309
So we see tight controls, we see restrictions
from the outside, and then finally, we see

00:19:10.309 --> 00:19:11.380
kind of a flooding.

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:15.350
So, positive messaging about the party and
what a great job the party is doing.

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:17.299
What a great job the government is doing.

00:19:17.299 --> 00:19:21.679
This is known as wumao, which is the 50 Cent
Army, which is how much that they were reportedly

00:19:21.679 --> 00:19:23.580
paid for each posting.

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:28.260
They would post positive things about Uncle
Xi and what a great job the Chinese Communist

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:29.260
party was doing.

00:19:29.260 --> 00:19:34.169
So a very, very tightly controlled internet
cutoff for the most part for internal users

00:19:34.169 --> 00:19:35.539
from the rest of the world.

00:19:35.539 --> 00:19:40.110
SIERRA: To recap, the Chinese Internet model
shows that it’s possible to control what

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:45.030
a large population is able to see online,
while also regulating what they’re able

00:19:45.030 --> 00:19:46.169
to say there.

00:19:46.169 --> 00:19:49.860
It’s tightly restricted, and rife with propaganda.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:54.280
Interestingly though, it does share one common
denominator with the American Internet - in

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:57.480
both places citizen’s data is up for grabs.

00:19:57.480 --> 00:20:02.669
In The U.S., it’s exploited by corporations,
whereas in China, it’s exploited by the

00:20:02.669 --> 00:20:03.669
government.

00:20:03.669 --> 00:20:06.309
And that leads us to the final model: Europe.

00:20:06.309 --> 00:20:13.610
WION: The European Union is now proposing
to impose hefty fines to keep the tech giants

00:20:13.610 --> 00:20:14.610
in check.

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:16.270
Channel 4 News: Say hello to GDPR.

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:20.730
It's being described as the biggest shake
up of data protection laws in a generation,

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:24.700
giving ordinary people unprecedented control
over the information companies’ hold on

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:25.700
us.

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:28.260
CNBC: We’ve seen anti-trust action from
the European commission.

00:20:28.260 --> 00:20:30.950
They fined google a combined 9.5 billion dollars.

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:36.070
TRT World Now: European Union has threatened
to ban Twitter in its territory unless the

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:40.039
company’s owner, Elon Musk, makes sure that
Twitter abides by its laws on content moderation.

00:20:40.039 --> 00:20:45.570
SEGAL: What happened very quickly in Europe
was that they decided privacy, data privacy,

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:46.630
was a human right.

00:20:46.630 --> 00:20:49.040
Again, a lot of this came out of history.

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:53.520
It came out of World War II and Germany was
a leader on this, that the state collecting

00:20:53.520 --> 00:20:58.540
data on you is a step towards fascism and
authoritarian states.

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:01.700
And the technology companies having this kind
of data is also.

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:07.919
So very early on, the Europeans said, well,
we want to regulate how that data's collected,

00:21:07.919 --> 00:21:13.480
who has access to it, and to put more restrictions
on it than the United States had on those

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:14.480
provisions.

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:18.640
Now, the Europeans have always said, ‘we
want to encourage innovation and growth,’

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:21.870
but the regulatory environment never really
seemed to grow, right?

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:25.990
It's very hard to think of Big Tech platforms
that came out of Europe.

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:29.019
Spotify is one, Skype is another.

00:21:29.019 --> 00:21:32.520
But compared to what came out of Silicon Valley,
it’s much, much less.

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:39.970
BRADFORD: So the EU then really became globally
known as the privacy regulator, when it adopted

00:21:39.970 --> 00:21:44.960
the general data privacy regulation, the GDPR
in 2016.

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:50.190
So that is a major piece of regulation focused
on enhancing user privacy, and then restraining

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:55.180
what is known as surveillance capitalism,
the way the platform companies are exploiting

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:56.970
the user's data.

00:21:56.970 --> 00:22:01.840
SIERRA: You know those terms of service agreements
that you always sign but never read?

00:22:01.840 --> 00:22:07.230
Accepting them often gives private companies
access to an extraordinary amount of data

00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:08.230
on your phone.

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:12.820
It’s an open secret - roughly 60 percent
of Americans believe that it’s not possible

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:17.450
to go through a day without having their data
tracked in one way or another.

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:21.330
And on this front, Europe has pushed back
much harder than the U.S.

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:24.309
BRADFORD: So, the privacy has certainly been
one issue.

00:22:24.309 --> 00:22:31.600
But the EU has also been very focused on deploying
antitrust laws to reduce the power of the

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:32.680
largest platform.

00:22:32.680 --> 00:22:36.340
So that is another element of that regulatory
approach.

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:38.410
And then third, I would say content moderation.

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:44.549
So there's been various voluntary codes, on
mitigating hate speech and disinformation

00:22:44.549 --> 00:22:48.470
that the EU has signed with the leading tech
platforms.

00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:54.140
And then very recently, so this year, there
was a major piece of legislation that was

00:22:54.140 --> 00:22:59.340
adopted, the Digital Services Act, that then
takes this content moderation regulation to

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:00.400
another level.

00:23:00.400 --> 00:23:07.360
So the EU is setting binding rules on how
to improve accountability and transparency,

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:11.230
when it comes to content moderation by these
leading platforms.

00:23:11.230 --> 00:23:18.980
SIERRA: So if I'm browsing Facebook, which
is an American company, but I'm in the EU,

00:23:18.980 --> 00:23:23.731
my privacy would be better protected there,
even though it's from an American company

00:23:23.731 --> 00:23:25.450
that doesn't have the same rules?

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:30.450
SEGAL: Yeah, so Facebook operating in Europe
has to abide by those rules.

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:35.460
And so part of those rules are, for example,
there's a right to be forgotten in Europe.

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:41.900
So as a teenager, you might have done something
really dumb, it got posted to Instagram and

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:46.020
it's still up there and you are applying for
jobs now, and you're afraid it's going to

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:47.039
be up there.

00:23:47.039 --> 00:23:48.779
In Europe you have the right to ask for that
to be taken out.

00:23:48.779 --> 00:23:49.779
SIERRA: Oh, that's nice.

00:23:49.779 --> 00:23:50.779
SEGAL: Right?

00:23:50.779 --> 00:23:55.630
And so if you're Google or Facebook and there's
a court that rules that if it's a legitimate

00:23:55.630 --> 00:23:57.679
reason to take it down, then they'll take
it down.

00:23:57.679 --> 00:23:59.970
We don't have that right in the United States.

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:03.960
We're all now partly seeing that right now,
every website we go to there's a pop-up that

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:07.489
asks you about the cookies that you're willing
and the data that you're willing to share.

00:24:07.489 --> 00:24:09.190
That's because of European law.

00:24:09.190 --> 00:24:13.810
So the operators of those websites either
are operating in Europe at the same time,

00:24:13.810 --> 00:24:18.720
and so they have to ensure that the data is
being either stored locally or how it's being

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:22.200
shared in the U.S. is equivalent to what happens
in Europe.

00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:24.300
Here’s how it works.

00:24:24.300 --> 00:24:30.779
Nations within the EU can fine companies up
to 4% of their annual revenue if they violate

00:24:30.779 --> 00:24:32.789
the rules of GDPR.

00:24:32.789 --> 00:24:36.530
And this applies to any U.S. company with
EU users.

00:24:36.530 --> 00:24:44.730
For example, in 2019, France fined Google
$57 million because they failed to obtain

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:47.720
consent for user’s personalized ads.

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:53.049
GDPR was a win for private users in Europe,
but it caused headaches for companies that

00:24:53.049 --> 00:24:56.440
built their business around user data and
targeted ads.

00:24:56.440 --> 00:25:02.529
WHEELER: No one paid attention in American
tech companies to GDPR until the day it was

00:25:02.529 --> 00:25:08.559
put into force and all of a sudden a bunch
of regulatory filings happened that meant

00:25:08.559 --> 00:25:13.399
that American companies could no longer transfer
information inside the European Union unless

00:25:13.399 --> 00:25:16.000
they abided by GDPR.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:21.740
And it was as if all of a sudden the eyes
of every C-level executive other than the

00:25:21.740 --> 00:25:26.140
CTO were opened unto the reality of the fact
that there's a world outside of the United

00:25:26.140 --> 00:25:31.000
States, and sometimes we have to do what they
want us to do if you want to sell in their

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:32.000
market.

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:36.990
SIERRA: On average, companies are estimated
to have paid more than $1.4 million dollars

00:25:36.990 --> 00:25:40.610
each, in order to come into compliance with
the new law.

00:25:40.610 --> 00:25:43.480
It was a costly development for the tech sector.

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:47.020
And some experts think that that may have
been the whole point.

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:51.779
WHEELER: Now, why would the European Union
be doing something like this?

00:25:51.779 --> 00:25:55.530
First, there's a good strong argument that
a lot of what they're doing is very important

00:25:55.530 --> 00:26:00.860
to make sure that they can regulate and handle
speech issues, privacy issues inside the European

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:04.520
Union that are not adequately compensated
for under American law.

00:26:04.520 --> 00:26:07.049
We don't have good privacy regulation in the
United States.

00:26:07.049 --> 00:26:10.649
It's being done state by state, California,
Colorado had that done state by state, but

00:26:10.649 --> 00:26:13.230
we don't have national privacy legislation.

00:26:13.230 --> 00:26:17.409
But there's also an underlying frustration,
I think, in the European Union over the fact

00:26:17.409 --> 00:26:20.630
that they don't have a native Apple, Google,
Facebook.

00:26:20.630 --> 00:26:24.809
There is no giant provider like that in the
European Union.

00:26:24.809 --> 00:26:29.200
And so there's a question about whether or
not the EU could regulate their way to having

00:26:29.200 --> 00:26:33.480
something like that by trying to level the
playing field or if they could just tax their

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:34.480
way to it.

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:38.930
So again, the question kind of comes to the
point here about whether or not the European

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:44.919
Union is taxing or regulating its way to trying
to be an equal player competitively or because

00:26:44.919 --> 00:26:48.049
there's an actual underlying element thereof
desiring to protect citizens.

00:26:48.049 --> 00:26:49.320
Could be both, could be neither.​​

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:55.310
SIERRA: So, perhaps the EU’s motivation
is not entirely based on personal privacy.

00:26:55.310 --> 00:27:00.700
But other analysts, including Anu, disagree
that regulations like GDPR are motivated by

00:27:00.700 --> 00:27:03.600
tech-sector jealousy, or competitive goals.

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:07.299
BRADFORD: The biggest tech companies happen
to be all American.

00:27:07.299 --> 00:27:12.760
But they also happen to be the biggest companies
that create the biggest problems in the digital

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:13.760
marketplace.

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:18.070
And if you look at the domain where the EU
has probably gone furthest in it’s regulation,

00:27:18.070 --> 00:27:23.170
antitrust, there's been three cases against
Google over 10 billion in fines, yes, the

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:25.370
EU was going after Google.

00:27:25.370 --> 00:27:27.059
But who brought the initial complaint?

00:27:27.059 --> 00:27:28.230
Tt was Microsoft.

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:30.929
Tt was another American company.

00:27:30.929 --> 00:27:35.429
There is no European search engine that the
Europeans are trying to protect when going

00:27:35.429 --> 00:27:36.429
after Facebook.

00:27:36.429 --> 00:27:41.970
Again, there is no European social media company
that is a rival to Facebook.

00:27:41.970 --> 00:27:47.710
Often it is other American companies challenging
the conduct of their competitors in the EU

00:27:47.710 --> 00:27:52.630
fighting the civil war in the European territory,
because there's nothing that they can get

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:53.730
from Washington.

00:27:53.730 --> 00:27:58.269
SIERRA: Whether you believe that the EU’s
privacy laws were created for the protection

00:27:58.269 --> 00:28:04.100
of the people or just to be a thorn in the
side of big American tech, these restrictions

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:06.250
mean a different internet environment.

00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:10.090
And whatever you think about it, Europe is
showing the world that it’s possible to

00:28:10.090 --> 00:28:13.330
have the Internet and a bit of privacy too.

00:28:13.330 --> 00:28:16.620
SIERRA: So what, does the future hold?

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:19.919
Who will come out on top of the three internets?

00:28:19.919 --> 00:28:22.779
SEGAL: I don't know if there's going to be
a winner.

00:28:22.779 --> 00:28:25.380
I think there's going to be spheres of influence.

00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:30.919
I think there's a lot of authoritarian developing
states that are going to find parts of the

00:28:30.919 --> 00:28:32.419
Chinese model attractive.

00:28:32.419 --> 00:28:36.390
The Chinese have the resources to help them
build it, they're training, they're bringing

00:28:36.390 --> 00:28:38.049
people over.

00:28:38.049 --> 00:28:40.590
The big issue is going to be kind of the developing
south.

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:46.549
And in my mind it's going to be driven mostly
by capacity, and capacity building.

00:28:46.549 --> 00:28:52.049
SIERRA: The developing south, which includes
Africa, much of the Middle East, Latin America,

00:28:52.049 --> 00:28:57.029
and Southeast Asia, has been turning to China
for help building roads, bridges and other

00:28:57.029 --> 00:29:00.110
infrastructure as part of the Belt and Road
initiative.

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:03.460
But it’s not just brick-and-mortar development.

00:29:03.460 --> 00:29:09.899
In 2015, Beijing launched the Digital Silk
Road project, which focuses on providing digital

00:29:09.899 --> 00:29:12.390
infrastructure to the same regions.

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:18.220
Under DSR, countries get help with things
like telecommunications, artificial intelligence,

00:29:18.220 --> 00:29:19.809
e-commerce and surveillance.

00:29:19.809 --> 00:29:26.029
In return, China gets to expand its global
tech footprint, promote tech-based authoritarianism,

00:29:26.029 --> 00:29:29.740
and gain access to a far larger pool of user
data.

00:29:29.740 --> 00:29:35.330
At least 16 developing countries have publicly
joined in, but the actual number could be

00:29:35.330 --> 00:29:37.130
as high as 138.

00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:43.440
SEGAL: So the Chinese in some ways have a
leg up because they're building the infrastructure,

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:44.440
right?

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:50.080
And while we saw, for example, Facebook talking
about building infrastructure in India and

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:53.970
other places, the way that they wanted to
do it alienated everybody.

00:29:53.970 --> 00:29:59.890
So the U.S. has to figure out how do we compete
on infrastructure to have a chance that more

00:29:59.890 --> 00:30:02.890
countries adopt that model.

00:30:02.890 --> 00:30:08.730
SIERRA: So I've heard about a Tech Cold War
with China.

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:10.850
What impact is that having?

00:30:10.850 --> 00:30:17.250
SEGAL: Yeah, so the Tech Cold War idea is
kind of a militarizing of this idea of different

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:20.700
models, and the threat of Chinese technology
itself.

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:26.730
So as Chinese tech companies globalized and
the social media platforms globalized, the

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:31.270
U.S. became increasingly worried about, oh,
did that create vulnerabilities in U.S. systems?

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:33.309
And who had access to the data?

00:30:33.309 --> 00:30:37.539
The first and kind of archetypal example of
this was a company called Huawei, which is

00:30:37.539 --> 00:30:39.080
a Chinese telecom provider.

00:30:39.080 --> 00:30:44.179
And in particular Huawei was the leader on
5G, the fifth generation of telecom so far

00:30:44.179 --> 00:30:48.840
for our cell phones, which is going to be
faster and have lower latency and allow self-driving

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:50.529
cars and all these other things.

00:30:50.529 --> 00:30:54.649
Now, the U.S. doesn't produce 5G, we don't
have any telecom equipment manufacturers.

00:30:54.649 --> 00:30:58.910
So there's Huawei and then there's two European
companies, Nokia and Ericsson.

00:30:58.910 --> 00:31:04.600
And Huawei was expanding very quickly globally,
it's cheaper than Nokia and Ericsson, and

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:08.711
the United States basically started saying,
look, if you use Huawei, you're going to have

00:31:08.711 --> 00:31:14.659
some real cybersecurity risks, because the
Chinese government can turn to Huawei and

00:31:14.659 --> 00:31:18.410
say, turn it off or disrupt it or hand us
the data.

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:22.470
And so the U.S. kind of started campaigning
about ripping Huawei out of the US.

00:31:22.470 --> 00:31:28.160
So some telecoms, particularly in the interior
of the U.S., was using Huawei, blocking Huawei,

00:31:28.160 --> 00:31:31.929
you can't buy a Huawei handset, things like
that in the United States.

00:31:31.929 --> 00:31:34.809
And then trying to convince other countries
not to use Huawei.

00:31:34.809 --> 00:31:39.539
So Canada used Huawei, the U.K. used Huawei,
a lot of our European allies in the U.S.,

00:31:39.539 --> 00:31:43.669
we really mounted this large campaign against
Huawei.

00:31:43.669 --> 00:31:48.220
So that started to have some effect in the
Trump administration, countries for a number

00:31:48.220 --> 00:31:52.470
of reasons, started saying, all right, we're
going to block Huawei from this next generation.

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:53.470
Now we see it with TikTok.

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:56.059
SIERRA: I was going to say it sounds similar
to the worries with TikTok?

00:31:56.059 --> 00:32:01.669
SEGAL: So TikTok, the worries are more and
more people are using it and you put that

00:32:01.669 --> 00:32:06.799
app on your phone and TikTok like all apps,
gets a lot of access to the data on your phone,

00:32:06.799 --> 00:32:07.799
right?

00:32:07.799 --> 00:32:11.470
Your location, your camera, possibly your
address book.

00:32:11.470 --> 00:32:16.320
So there are concerns that it allows the Chinese
to do intelligence gathering, although I don't

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:21.440
think that's the primary concern, but there's
also a concern about what we would call ‘influence

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:22.440
operations.’

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:27.169
So with TikTok, we don't really know why we
see the videos we see.

00:32:27.169 --> 00:32:32.190
The algorithm learns what you like and it
serves you up things.

00:32:32.190 --> 00:32:37.070
So there's concern again, even though TikTok
says no, we've separated from ByteDance, the

00:32:37.070 --> 00:32:42.399
Chinese company, that the Chinese can influence
stories that we see.

00:32:42.399 --> 00:32:47.889
There were some cases, for example, in Hong
Kong when there were the protests about democracy,

00:32:47.889 --> 00:32:51.720
they were very hard to find any of those stories
on TikTok.

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:55.679
So we now see a lot of concern about those
things and the U.S. is trying to convince

00:32:55.679 --> 00:32:58.080
other countries to do the same thing.

00:32:58.080 --> 00:33:01.919
Now again, the Chinese have always said, oh,
we know the U.S. intelligence services are

00:33:01.919 --> 00:33:06.110
cooperating with the U.S. tech companies,
and so it's safer for us to start taking those

00:33:06.110 --> 00:33:08.640
companies out of our systems.

00:33:08.640 --> 00:33:12.779
So we see China trying to get rid of Cisco
and Qualcomm and the other ones.

00:33:12.779 --> 00:33:16.890
And as we talked about at the beginning, they've
always blocked U.S. social media.

00:33:16.890 --> 00:33:22.420
So we are beginning to see a kind of divergence
of those stacks, and we're beginning to see

00:33:22.420 --> 00:33:28.180
Washington and Beijing reaching out to others
saying, ‘you guys should choose one of us,’

00:33:28.180 --> 00:33:29.940
which the other countries don't want to do.

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:31.120
SIERRA: Right.

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:33.110
Well what about everyone else?

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:37.510
Especially big countries, you know, you mentioned
India or Brazil, where are they headed and

00:33:37.510 --> 00:33:41.760
do they have to pick one of the, I guess,
three to join?

00:33:41.760 --> 00:33:42.760
SEGAL: Yeah.

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:48.029
I think the big countries really don't want
to choose and will probably pick and choose.

00:33:48.029 --> 00:33:55.110
So India for example, blocked TikTok and banned
TikTok and almost 200 other Chinese apps,

00:33:55.110 --> 00:33:59.990
after the conflict in the border in the Himalayas,
and so to punish the Chinese, and they were

00:33:59.990 --> 00:34:02.660
also concerned about information gathering
and data.

00:34:02.660 --> 00:34:08.200
But we also see that the Indians have a lot
of concerns about communal violence and blasphemy

00:34:08.200 --> 00:34:09.200
and other things.

00:34:09.200 --> 00:34:14.889
And so they are looking to more tightly regulate
the internet than U.S. companies are comfortable

00:34:14.889 --> 00:34:15.889
with.

00:34:15.889 --> 00:34:20.210
And the Indians want to inspect both Chinese
product and U.S. product.

00:34:20.210 --> 00:34:25.960
They have a strong and long tradition of technological,
or seeking technological autonomy, and don't

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:27.550
really want to do that.

00:34:27.550 --> 00:34:32.490
So I think India, Brazil, Indonesia, South
Africa, they're gonna pick and choose.

00:34:32.490 --> 00:34:37.860
SIERRA: Back when it launched, the internet
seemed limitless and borderless.

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:44.330
But that vision has given way to an era of
silos, one that is reinforcing regional information

00:34:44.330 --> 00:34:48.450
bubbles, and driving us further and further
apart in our digital lives.

00:34:48.450 --> 00:34:52.419
SIERRA: Do you see this splintering as having
been inevitable?

00:34:52.419 --> 00:34:59.490
SEGAL: I do, because the internet really was
a kind of creation of a unique culture.

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:04.310
We talk about Silicon Valley, but this combination
of free speech, the First Amendment and a

00:35:04.310 --> 00:35:07.470
very light regulatory hand, is really very,
very American.

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:10.490
That just doesn't exist anywhere else in the
world.

00:35:10.490 --> 00:35:14.990
And so of course when other countries were
confronted with this platform, they brought

00:35:14.990 --> 00:35:16.961
different political concerns to it.

00:35:16.961 --> 00:35:21.920
So, it always struck me from the beginning
that of course, countries were going to try

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:22.920
to regulate.

00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:27.820
And there were many people in the nineties
and the two-thousands that wrote about this.

00:35:27.820 --> 00:35:29.760
They just weren't the dominant voices.

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:33.310
People were kind of like, ‘Oh, you political
scientists, what do you know?

00:35:33.310 --> 00:35:34.420
What’re you talking about?

00:35:34.420 --> 00:35:39.490
Or you're so narrow minded, you don't see
the liberatory potential of it.’

00:35:39.490 --> 00:35:45.589
The debate in the United States has really
shifted and soured around 2016.

00:35:45.589 --> 00:35:50.869
But before that, we really had a much more
optimistic view about the internet and the

00:35:50.869 --> 00:35:52.260
technology companies themselves.

00:35:52.260 --> 00:35:57.170
WHEELER: The downsides of a splintered internet
go to the same downsides of any situation

00:35:57.170 --> 00:35:58.369
where there's partisanship.

00:35:58.369 --> 00:36:02.290
We've all seen the pain and problems that
partisanship in the United States causes us.

00:36:02.290 --> 00:36:05.950
A lot of it has to do with a lack of the capacity
to understand and accept reality.

00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:11.200
If you've been taught that a different reality
exists, and if you shut your mind to the possibility

00:36:11.200 --> 00:36:15.880
of an alternate point of view, how do you
come to a shared understanding of what geopolitical

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:20.140
concerns are, what rights are, what a person
is owed by their government and what we owe

00:36:20.140 --> 00:36:21.140
to each other?

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:23.200
How do you even talk about reality?

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:28.580
The number of people who don't know that the
Tiananmen Square protests existed in China,

00:36:28.580 --> 00:36:31.119
or who genuinely believe that it was all mocked
up.

00:36:31.119 --> 00:36:34.290
The number of people who equate those two
things in China is large.

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:36.410
So how do you get to that shared sense of
reality?

00:36:36.410 --> 00:36:40.579
The more we see that splintering, the consequence
of this, the more we're going to see a differentiation

00:36:40.579 --> 00:36:43.930
in reality because information is how we make
our reality.

00:36:43.930 --> 00:36:45.870
So that's where I think we're going with this.

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:47.881
Just in case you wanted to end on this super
depressing note.

00:36:47.881 --> 00:36:48.881
SIERRA: That was awesome ... Thank you.

00:36:48.881 --> 00:36:49.881
Yeah.

00:36:49.881 --> 00:36:50.881
WHEELER: You're welcome.

00:36:50.881 --> 00:36:51.881
No problem.

00:36:51.881 --> 00:36:52.881
Absolutely no problem.

00:36:52.881 --> 00:36:53.881
SIERRA: And we’re all doomed.

00:36:53.881 --> 00:37:04.940
WHEELER: I'm here for your downers all day
long.

